{0 AWS Guides}

Practical guides for using the aws package. See the
{{!page-index}index page} for the full library listing.

{1 Design philosophy}

This package is built around a {b pure core / impure edge} split:

{ul
  {li {b Pure core} — signing, credentials, endpoints, error types, request and
    response types. No IO, no OS dependencies. Testable with plain unit tests.}
  {li {b Impure edge} — runtime adapters that actually make HTTP calls. You
    pick one adapter at the application boundary and pass it in.}
}

The {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} module type is the seam between the two.
Service packages depend on this abstraction, not on any particular concurrency
library. That means the same S3 client code works with Eio, Lwt, or a custom
adapter — you just wire it up differently at the edge.

{1 Request signing}

Every AWS API request must be signed — this is how AWS authenticates callers.
AWS Signature Version 4 is implemented in {!module:Awskit.Signing}.

{[
let credentials = Awskit.Credentials.make
  ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
  ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
  ()

let signed = Awskit.Signing.sign_request
  ~credentials
  ~region:"us-east-1"
  ~service:"s3"
  ~meth:"GET"
  ~path:"/my-bucket/my-key"
  ~query:""
  ~headers:[("host", "s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com")]
  ~payload:""
  ~now:(Ptime.v (2024, 1, 15, ((10, 30, 0), 0)))
in
(* signed.headers contains the Authorization header,
   x-amz-date, and x-amz-content-sha256 *)
]}

The [headers] field in the result includes the [Authorization] header,
[x-amz-date], and [x-amz-content-sha256]. The [signed_headers_str] field is the
semicolon-separated list of signed header names (used in the canonical request).

Signing is {b entirely pure} — no IO, no network. You can test it with plain
unit tests, no mocks needed. Service packages handle signing automatically; the
raw API is available for custom AWS services or lower-level use.

{2 Utility functions}

{!module:Awskit.Signing} also exposes helpers used internally and occasionally
useful directly:
{ul
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.uri_encode} — URI-encode per AWS rules. Pass
    [~encode_slash:false] for paths (slashes are not encoded).}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.canonical_query} — Sort query parameters by name and
    URI-encode keys and values.}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.ptime_to_date_time} — Split a [Ptime.t] into a
    datestamp ["YYYYMMDD"] and an [x-amz-date] ["YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ"].}
}

{1 Credentials}

{2 Permanent credentials}

{[
let creds = Awskit.Credentials.make
  ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
  ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
  ()
]}

Credentials are opaque — the record fields are not accessible directly. Use
{!val:Awskit.Credentials.access_key_id} and
{!val:Awskit.Credentials.session_token} to read non-secret fields. The secret
access key is accepted at construction time but is not exposed afterward, which
helps prevent accidental logging.

{2 Temporary credentials (STS, IAM roles)}

When [session_token] is present, {!val:Awskit.Signing.sign_request}
automatically adds the [x-amz-security-token] header:

{[
let temp_creds = Awskit.Credentials.make
  ~access_key_id:"ASIATESTSESSIONTOKEN"
  ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
  ~session_token:(Some "FwoGZXIvYXdzEBY...")
  ()
]}

{2 Loading from environment variables}

{!module:Awskit_unix.Credentials} reads the standard AWS environment variables:

{[
let credentials = Awskit_unix.Credentials.from_env () |> Result.get_ok
(* Reads AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID (required),
   AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (required),
   AWS_SESSION_TOKEN (optional, for temporary credentials) *)
]}

Returns [Error (`Invalid_request msg)] if a required variable is missing or
empty. This is the simplest way to get credentials in development — set the
environment variables and call [from_env].

{1 Endpoints}

{!module:Awskit.Endpoint} constructs endpoint URLs. Runtime adapters build these
automatically from the region, but you can override them for S3-compatible
services:

{[
let standard = Awskit.Endpoint.https ~host:"s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com" ()
let localstack = Awskit.Endpoint.http ~host:"localhost" ~port:4566 ()
]}

The {!val:Awskit.Endpoint.create} function gives full control over scheme, host,
and port. Use {!val:Awskit.Endpoint.to_url_prefix} to get the full URL prefix
(e.g., ["https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com"]) and
{!val:Awskit.Endpoint.authority} for the [host:port] string.

{1 Choosing an adapter}

The {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} module type defines the interface between
pure code and impure adapters. Each adapter provides a monad type and a
connection type. You pick one adapter and it works for {b every} service
package — there is no per-service setup.

{2 Eio (OCaml 5)}

Direct-style on algebraic effects. [type 'a t = 'a], errors are
[result] values. This is the recommended adapter for new OCaml 5 projects:

{[
let conn = Awskit_eio.create
  ~env ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
]}

The monad type is [type 'a t = 'a] — [let*] is just [let]. No promises, no
callbacks. Errors surface as [(result, Error.t)] return values.

{2 Lwt + Unix}

For existing Lwt codebases. Uses promises:

{[
let conn = Awskit_lwt_unix.create
  ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
|> Result.get_ok
]}

The monad type is [type 'a t = 'a Lwt.t] — use [let*] for binding.
Optional parameters include custom TLS ([?ctx]), clock ([?clock]), explicit
endpoint overrides ([?endpoint]), and a buffered response size limit
([?max_response_body_bytes]).

If arguments are omitted, [awskit.lwt_unix] reads only these standard AWS
environment variables:

{[
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
AWS_REGION
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
]}

Endpoint overrides are explicit; [awskit.lwt_unix] does not read endpoint URLs
from the environment.

{2 Lwt (generic)}

For MirageOS or custom HTTP backends. Applies the runtime functor to any
[Cohttp_lwt.S.Client] implementation:

{[
module My_runtime = Awskit_lwt.Make (Cohttp_lwt_unix.Client)
let conn = My_runtime.create ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials
  ~clock:(fun () -> Ptime_clock.now ()) ()
]}

This is lower-level. You supply the HTTP client, the clock, and any explicit
endpoint override. Most users should prefer {!module:Awskit_lwt_unix}.

{1 Custom endpoints}

Override the endpoint to point at a local S3-compatible service (LocalStack,
MinIO, etc.):

{[
(* Eio with custom endpoint *)
let conn = Awskit_eio.create
  ~env ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials
  ~endpoint:(Awskit.Endpoint.http ~host:"localhost" ~port:4566 ())
  ()
]}

{[
(* Lwt with custom endpoint *)
let conn = Awskit_lwt_unix.create
  ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials
  ~endpoint:(Awskit.Endpoint.http ~host:"localhost" ~port:9000 ())
  ()
|> Result.get_ok
]}

Pass an explicit {!module:Awskit.Endpoint} when talking to LocalStack, MinIO,
or other S3-compatible services. Omit [?endpoint] for production AWS HTTPS
endpoints.

{1 Error handling}

Errors are represented as polymorphic variants that compose across layers.
The base type from {!module:Awskit.Error}:

{[
type base =
  [ `Access_denied
  | `Service_unavailable of string
  | `Request_error of int * string        (* status, body *)
  | `Body_read_error of string
  | `Invalid_response of string
  | `Invalid_header of string * string     (* name, value *)
  | `Missing_response_header of string
  | `Invalid_xml of string
  | `Invalid_json of string
  | `Invalid_request of string ]
]}

Service packages extend this with service-specific variants. For example,
{{:../awskit-s3/index.html}[awskit-s3]} adds [\`Not_found],
[\`Precondition_failed], [\`Bucket_already_exists], and more.

Pattern match on specific errors, catch a group, or handle everything with a
wildcard:

{[
match result with
| Ok value -> (* success *)
| Error `Access_denied -> (* handle 403 *)
| Error (#Awskit.Error.base as e) -> (* any other base error *)
]}

Because the variants are polymorphic, you can write handlers that work across
multiple service packages — the base cases always apply.

{1 Writing a custom adapter}

Implement {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} for your platform. The module type
has two parts:

{2 The monad type ['a t]}

For Eio, [type 'a t = 'a] — direct-style. For Lwt,
[type 'a t = 'a Lwt.t]. The monad abstraction lets service packages work
with either without knowing which one they're using. You must provide
{!val:Awskit.Runtime.S.return} and {!val:Awskit.Runtime.S.bind}.

{2 The connection type}

The [connection] type bundles everything needed to make AWS calls:
{ul
  {li [region] — AWS region (e.g., ["us-east-1"])}
  {li [credentials] — AWS credentials for signing requests}
  {li [clock] — Time source for request timestamps and presigned URL expiry}
  {li [endpoint] — Optional explicit endpoint override (for LocalStack,
    MinIO, custom endpoints)}
}

{2 The [call] function}

The [call] function is the {b only IO operation} in the interface. It takes
a {!type:Awskit.Request.t} and returns a {!type:Awskit.Response.t} (or an error).
Everything else in the system is pure.

{2 When to write a custom adapter}

{ul
  {li {b Testing} — use a simulated network (see
    {{:../awskit-s3/guides.html}[awskit-s3 Sim]} for a built-in example)}
  {li {b JavaScript targets} — use the browser [fetch] API}
  {li {b MirageOS unikernels} — use a Mirage-specific HTTP client}
  {li {b Observability} — inject logging, tracing, or metrics middleware}
}

{1 The Request type}

{!module:Awskit.Request} is the HTTP request type that service packages build and
runtime adapters send. It is exposed for custom adapters:

{[
type meth = GET | PUT | POST | DELETE | HEAD

type t = {
  meth : meth;
  host : string;
  port : int option;
  path : string;          (* includes query string *)
  headers : (string * string) list;
  body : string;
}
]}

Use {!val:Awskit.Request.meth_to_string} to convert the method to its HTTP
string representation.

{1 The Response type}

{!module:Awskit.Response} wraps an HTTP response with case-insensitive header
lookup and typed accessors:

{[
type t = {
  status : int;
  headers : (string * string) list;
  body : string;
}
]}

Helpers:
{ul
  {li {!val:Awskit.Response.is_success} — status 2xx}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Response.header} — case-insensitive lookup, returns [None] if
    absent}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Response.header_exn} — required header, returns [Error] if
    missing or empty}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Response.header_int} — optional integer header}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Response.header_int_exn} — required integer header}
}
